{"subjectId":"python","id":"lists-tuples","title":"Lists & Tuples","group":"Data Structures","order":5,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>list</code> is a mutable, ordered, dynamic array. <code>tuple</code> is its immutable sibling — lighter, hashable, and used for fixed records.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>list</strong> — a dynamic array of <em>references</em> to objects. Indexing is O(1); appending is amortised O(1) because Python over-allocates capacity.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-clock\"></i>Core operations &amp; complexity</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Example</th><th>Time</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Index / assign</td><td><code>xs[i]</code>, <code>xs[i] = v</code></td><td>O(1)</td></tr><tr><td>Append / pop from end</td><td><code>xs.append(v)</code>, <code>xs.pop()</code></td><td>O(1) amortised</td></tr><tr><td>Insert / pop at front</td><td><code>xs.insert(0, v)</code>, <code>xs.pop(0)</code></td><td><strong>O(n)</strong> — use <code>deque</code></td></tr><tr><td>Search</td><td><code>v in xs</code>, <code>xs.index(v)</code></td><td>O(n)</td></tr><tr><td>Slice</td><td><code>xs[a:b]</code></td><td>O(b − a)</td></tr><tr><td>Sort</td><td><code>xs.sort()</code>, <code>sorted(xs)</code></td><td>O(n log n) — Timsort, stable</td></tr><tr><td>Extend</td><td><code>xs.extend(ys)</code>, <code>xs + ys</code></td><td>O(len(ys))</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>xs = [5, 2, 9]\nxs.append(1)\nxs.extend([7, 3])\nxs.insert(1, 4)\nprint(xs)                       # [5, 4, 2, 9, 1, 7, 3]\nprint(xs.pop(), xs.pop(0))      # 3 5\nxs.remove(9)                    # removes the first 9 (ValueError if absent)\nprint(sorted(xs), xs[::-1])     # [1, 2, 4, 7] [7, 1, 2, 4]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-sort-ascending\"></i><code>sort()</code> vs <code>sorted()</code></h3>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-sort\"></i><code>xs.sort()</code></h3><p>Sorts <strong>in place</strong> and returns <code>None</code>. Only for lists.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-copy\"></i><code>sorted(it)</code></h3><p>Returns a <strong>new</strong> list from any iterable. The original is untouched.</p></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>people = [(\"Ravi\", 30), (\"Asha\", 25), (\"Kiran\", 30)]\nprint(sorted(people, key=lambda p: p[1]))\n# [('Asha', 25), ('Ravi', 30), ('Kiran', 30)]   ← stable: Ravi stays before Kiran\nprint(sorted(people, key=lambda p: (-p[1], p[0])))\n# [('Kiran', 30), ('Ravi', 30), ('Asha', 25)]   ← age desc, then name asc</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-copy\"></i>Copies: aliasing, shallow, deep</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import copy\ngrid = [[0, 0], [0, 0]]\nalias = grid                 # same list\nshallow = grid[:]            # new outer list, same inner lists\ndeep = copy.deepcopy(grid)   # everything copied\ngrid[0][0] = 9\nprint(alias[0][0], shallow[0][0], deep[0][0])   # 9 9 0</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Classic bug</div><p><code>[[0] * 3] * 3</code> creates <strong>three references to the same inner list</strong> — changing one row changes all. Build 2-D lists with a comprehension: <code>[[0] * 3 for _ in range(3)]</code>.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>bad = [[0] * 3] * 3\nbad[0][0] = 1\nprint(bad)                              # [[1, 0, 0], [1, 0, 0], [1, 0, 0]]\ngood = [[0] * 3 for _ in range(3)]\ngood[0][0] = 1\nprint(good)                             # [[1, 0, 0], [0, 0, 0], [0, 0, 0]]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-brackets\"></i>Tuples, packing &amp; unpacking</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>point = (3, 4)\nx, y = point                    # unpacking\na, b = 1, 2\na, b = b, a                     # swap without a temp variable\nfirst, *middle, last = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\nprint(x, y, a, b)               # 3 4 2 1\nprint(first, middle, last)      # 1 [2, 3, 4] 5\nsingle = (42,)                  # the comma makes the tuple, not the parentheses\nprint(type(single).__name__)    # tuple</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>list</code></th><th><code>tuple</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mutable</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Hashable (dict key / set item)</td><td>No</td><td>Yes, if its items are hashable</td></tr><tr><td>Memory / speed</td><td>Slightly larger</td><td>Smaller, slightly faster to create</td></tr><tr><td>Use for</td><td>Collections that grow and change</td><td>Fixed records: coordinates, DB rows, dict keys</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-tag\"></i>Named tuples</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from collections import namedtuple\nPoint = namedtuple(\"Point\", [\"x\", \"y\"])\np = Point(3, 4)\nprint(p.x + p.y, p)          # 7 Point(x=3, y=4)</code></pre></figure></section>"}